:question/problem "Consider the following system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, we wish to find a particular solution in the form ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t),$ where $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system and $u(t)$ is a column vector of functions to be determined. A derivation of the formula for ${x}_{p}(t)$ is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φu(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}^{′}(t)=Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)=AΦu(t)+f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ ${Φ}^{′}=AΦ$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $Φ{u}^{′}(t)=f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${u}^{′}(t)={Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ $u(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by {{field-1}} both sides of  the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by {{field-2}} both sides of the equation.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting {{field-3}} into {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ To get $\\text{L2},$ we are $\\text{differentiating}$ with respect to $t$ both sides of the equation in $\\text{L1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}^{′}(t) & =(Φu(t))^{′} \\\\ {x}_{p}^{′}(t) & =Φ{u}^{′}(t)+{Φ}^{′}u(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ To get $\\text{L7},$ we are $\\text{integrating}$ both sides of the equation in $\\text{L6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}(t)f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L1},$ $\\text{L7},$ and $\\text{L8}.$ Substituting\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L7}$ into $\\text{L1},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φu(t) \\\\  & =Φ\\int {Φ}^{-1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L8}$ is obtained by substituting $\\text{L7}$ into $\\text{L1}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d23f2a1-55fa-5398-9016-dc6fbc55c97b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9705533-e0fc-5e2f-b307-0391172d952b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8e^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9705533-e0fc-5e2f-b307-0391172d952b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8e^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370963} :answer-field/id #uuid "31dba337-28f6-5aca-9204-9834bcc8483b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370962} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c510ed8d-43e1-5e59-afd5-2fc574b43aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c510ed8d-43e1-5e59-afd5-2fc574b43aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370965} :answer-field/id #uuid "b458c7db-a6f4-595f-a4e2-f4080afe8b12" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4fc05ed-28f9-50f5-a2fb-760e91d4ee30" :question/math-academy-id "q-337230" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $2{e}^{-t}$ |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{4t}-0\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-t} \\\\ -8{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 2{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -8{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-t} \\\\ 2{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d23f2a1-55fa-5398-9016-dc6fbc55c97b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02933199-7613-5d8e-8527-93b5f5e310a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02933199-7613-5d8e-8527-93b5f5e310a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5e^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370968} :answer-field/id #uuid "438186cf-e28f-55e2-bc0a-165746ccd322" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370967} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa8feda5-bd99-5f75-a416-17048daba91b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa8feda5-bd99-5f75-a416-17048daba91b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370970} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce28f38f-e98f-5240-8eab-696979a64884" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3c1ca24-1032-5ae4-8b6d-0320c87ffa14" :question/math-academy-id "q-337280" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & -3{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}$ |  |  | $\\frac{17}{6}$ |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & -3{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-3{e}^{4t})-3{e}^{4t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}}[\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{4t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-3t}}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{4t} & -3{e}^{4t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-4t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-4t} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 4{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 5{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{-t} & -3{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{17}{6}{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{17}{6}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d23f2a1-55fa-5398-9016-dc6fbc55c97b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1d22408-9a53-5312-be19-f8235d22d2c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{5}e^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1d22408-9a53-5312-be19-f8235d22d2c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22}{5}e^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370973} :answer-field/id #uuid "00889b2e-d5d0-5138-b077-912960e6b3d5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370972} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c0555de-f0d7-5158-a983-8a53fd561939" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c0555de-f0d7-5158-a983-8a53fd561939" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370975} :answer-field/id #uuid "18c3e95b-741a-5126-95b1-224f69f73413" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2857f53c-ce32-5579-ae3e-edbb18aabeb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-337281" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & -4{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $\\frac{3}{5}{e}^{4t}$ |  |  | $-5$ |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & -4{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0\\cdot (-4{e}^{-3t})-5{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {e}^{2t}}[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-3t} & -5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ -{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{t}}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-3t} & -5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ -{e}^{2t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-2t} & -5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-2t} & -5{e}^{-2t} \\\\ -{e}^{3t} & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{3}{5}{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{3}{20}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{2t} & -4{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{22}{5}{e}^{-t} \\\\ \\frac{3}{20}{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{t} \\\\ -5{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf0708fe-bd9c-5455-a9f9-7b4135fafe3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370978} {:answer/id #uuid "1058c831-1405-5fa1-a083-44f30c41b355" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1058c831-1405-5fa1-a083-44f30c41b355" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370979} :answer-field/id #uuid "3414a190-78a6-540d-bedd-6f1ed341aae0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af61cfa8-d3e0-5ba1-9907-4c274c821745" :question/math-academy-id "q-337286" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $23-15t$ |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & 3{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot 3{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{-4t}}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=4t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=4\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(4t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{4}{3}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t}(3t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}(-\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-t{e}^{t}+\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-t{e}^{t}+{e}^{t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(1-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & 3{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t}(3t-1) \\\\ {e}^{t}(1-t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}4(3t-1)+9(1-t) \\\\ 4(3t-1)+27(1-t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}3t+5 \\\\ 23-15t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3487218e-1a03-5769-a408-ca276a27d3b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3487218e-1a03-5769-a408-ca276a27d3b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370982} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bee8842-2972-59b0-a604-8fb394719907" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c9d5a60-2dd6-52cb-b5d2-1016201cd266" :question/math-academy-id "q-337288" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $6t-6$ |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-2t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-{e}^{-t}\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 3t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}-2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 3t{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int -2t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=-2t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-2\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -2t{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-2t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}\\cdot (-2\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-t{e}^{2t}+\\int {e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-t{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(1-2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=3t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v={e}^{t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(3t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-\\int {e}^{t}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =3t{e}^{t}-3{e}^{t} \\\\  & =3{e}^{t}(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}(1-2t) \\\\ 3{e}^{t}(t-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}(1-2t)+6(t-1) \\\\ 6(t-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4t-5 \\\\ 6t-6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe455121-f052-5722-a275-63c873463d8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21t+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe455121-f052-5722-a275-63c873463d8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21t+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370985} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8967ffe-ea15-59de-bc9f-24c3596e24ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "435c6319-4985-51ca-87c3-285497ad7b2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-337306" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $39t+23$ |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & 3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot 3{e}^{t}-{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{e}^{-2t}}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} & -{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}4t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=4t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=4\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =4t\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}\\cdot (4\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{4}{3}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t}(3t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-t)\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int (-{e}^{-t})\\cdot (-\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =t{e}^{-t}-\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-t}(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & 3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9}{e}^{3t}(3t-1) \\\\ {e}^{-t}(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}4(3t-1)+9(t+1) \\\\ 4(3t-1)+27(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}21t+5 \\\\ 39t+23\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74dbd777-f24e-5d23-b495-a56710d71f5a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89813649-c7dd-5dba-84e7-96ba01dffd96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370988} {:answer/id #uuid "860d81ff-7e8e-5210-ac25-9b816e957be3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12t-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89813649-c7dd-5dba-84e7-96ba01dffd96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12t+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370988} :answer-field/id #uuid "97d2da6a-930b-55ad-878b-2512bc59f88b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69e79418-c556-5772-9366-f70f716cc80e" :question/math-academy-id "q-337307" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations. Using the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n| $x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\,\\,\\frac{1}{9}$ |  | $30t+20$ |  | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nNotice that we are given that\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & 5{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-5{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-3t}}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -5{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{e}^{-2t}}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -5{e}^{t} \\\\ -{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =-\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -5{e}^{3t} \\\\ -{e}^{-t} & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating the components by parts, we get the following:\n\n- For the first component, let $u=\\frac{5}{2}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5}{2}t{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(\\frac{5}{2}t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\int \\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}\\cdot (\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{5}{6}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}t{e}^{3t}-\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t}(3t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, let $u=-\\frac{1}{2}t$ and $\\text{d}v={e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ and $v=-{e}^{-t}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =(-\\frac{1}{2}t)\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})-\\int -{e}^{-t}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{-t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}(t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t} & 5{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t} & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{18}{e}^{3t}(3t-1) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-t}(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}5(3t-1)+45(t+1) \\\\ 5(3t-1)+9(t+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}30t+20 \\\\ 12t+2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
